test-core	Portable package tests (core scope; the list and its guards live in scripts/portable-tests.sh)	2026-11-14T03:08:05.1260210Z portable-tests.sh:   TestWorkspaceSwitchRefusesAJunctionToOutside         [fixture] ticket111 AC#10, re-based by ticket 347 after a MEASURED correction. The case is AC#3(ii) with a REAL junction: on windows it builds one with cmd /c mklink /J and asserts the switch is refused (runs and passes here). What this row said until 347 - 'on POSIX there is nothing to refuse, because the detection is a Windows implementation (reparseComponents returns nil elsewhere)' - is now half false, and the half that is false is the one a reader would act on: (1) the DETECTION is no longer Windows-only, because ticket 347 landed risk.pathresolver_other.go's reparseComponents as the lstat symlink walk, so a symlink is denied on POSIX exactly as a junction is denied on Windows; (2) the case nevertheless STILL SKIPS on linux at paths_workspace_test.go:198, re-measured after 347, because that skip is a platform guard (runtime.GOOS != windows) and not a capability probe - no test starts running here, and the row accounts for exactly what it always did. What is stale is the sentence inside the skip, not the skip. OWNER of the POSIX half: ticket 347 landed the detection a POSIX equivalent needs, but the conversion - build a SYMLINK instead of a junction, and rewrite that message - lives in internal/tools/paths_workspace_test.go, a file ticket 347's write surface forbids, so the POSIX-equivalent criterion is NOT faked here and the conversion is dispatched separately. Re-verified against the compiled LINUX test binary every run: delete the skip and the case starts running here; delete the case and GUARD's staleness check goes red.
